-- 140 -- **An atomic ARRAY: one carrier, 256 counters, and every access an
-- atomic operation on one element.**
--
-- Until 2026-09-16 `atomic REGEL : [u32; 256]` checked clean at the indexed
-- update and the emitter refused the DECLARATION (`C001`, *"`atomic` of an
-- unresolvable type"*) -- so a program that needed one counter per rule wrote
-- 256 scalar atomics and a flat if-dispatch to pick between them. Measured in
-- a firewall written outside this tree (`messung/muse/OPUS-BERICHT-ATOMFELD.md`):
-- 5136 lines and 1797 ops for a single increment, instead of the ~60 lines here.
--
-- **What the lowering is.** `_Atomic uint32_t REGEL[256];` -- C11 6.7.2.4p3
-- puts the qualifier on the ELEMENT type (an `_Atomic` ARRAY type is a
-- constraint violation), so every element is an atomic object of its own and
-- `&REGEL[i]` is an `_Atomic uint32_t *`: the first argument of every
-- `atomic_*_explicit` generic, unchanged.
--
-- **What keeps it honest.** The goal theorem (`Zielsatz/Spec.lean`) proves
-- `RennfreiBis` for every carrier EXCEPT the atomics, and the exemption
-- (`Akzeptiert.lean`, `atomarB`) is one Bool per global carrier -- a table is
-- never exempt. This array is ONE carrier: it is named once in `effects`, once
-- in the footprint, once under `atomare`. Two things follow and both are
-- measured here:
--   * every access is an atomic operation -- the bare indexed read below
--     lowers to `atomic_load_explicit(&REGEL[r], …)`, never to a plain
--     `REGEL[r]` beside a CAS loop;
--   * the index is bounded before it is used. `M103` compares the declared
--     range against the length (`beispiele/gift/1042`, `1043`), and `N380`
--     refuses an access whose bound cannot be shown at all
--     (`beispiele/gift/1040`, `1041`).
--
-- The increment is a bounded CAS loop because the language forbids an
-- unbounded one, and `folge` carries the wrapping step because the emitter
-- reads exact ranges off a declaration and not off an `exchange` binder.
module beispiel140 {

pub atomic REGEL : [u32; 256] relaxed;

extern fn regel_streit() -> never effects { diverges };

impl fn folge(x : u32) -> u32
    costs <= 2 ops
{
    return x +% 1;
}

-- The increment: one element, one bounded compare-exchange loop.
pub impl fn zaehle_regel(r : u32 in 0 .. 255)
    effects { reads REGEL, writes REGEL }
    costs   <= 8 ops
{
    let alt : u32 = REGEL[r] exchange update(t)
        bounded 64 ops
        on_exceeded regel_streit
    { return folge(t); } publishes nothing;
}

-- The reader: a bare indexed read, and it is an explicit relaxed load.
pub impl fn lies_regel(r : u32 in 0 .. 255) -> u32
    effects { reads REGEL }
    costs   <= 4 ops
{
    let n : u32 = REGEL[r];
    return n;
}

-- The reset: a store into one element is a `publishstmt` like every other
-- store to an atomic (`SPRACHE.md` §11.3), and it carries no payload.
pub impl fn loesche_regel(r : u32 in 0 .. 255)
    effects { writes REGEL }
    costs   <= 4 ops
{
    REGEL[r] = 0 publishes nothing;
}

}
